ntial risk for  teachers because at this moment it's a  potential risk we don't know it for  certain because  we don't have the data to back that up  so we're sort of saying okay look  there is this potential danger but we  know this  other danger for children most  definitely exists so let's start up in a  gradual approach and build up  and continually reassess our strategies  will teachers  uh have negative outcomes from this into  some degree yes we have to be honest  about it  will teachers catch covet 19 if schools  reopen yes we just have to say that  saying anything otherwise is a lie as  our federal leaders go on television  if they just say oh it's no big deal no  one's going to suffer that's a lie  the approach that i feel has worked best  for me in the healthcare space  has been the most upfront that you can  be with  what you're doing why you're doing it  and how you will pivot  if information changes and once you can  get the people who you're treating  in this situation teachers on the same  page as to why you're making these  decisions  the more likely they are to to accept  the outcome and  i feel that the federal government the  media has done a really poor job  arming teachers with good information  about what risks they face what  mitigation strategies they can use  because some of the things listed in the  cdc manuals  are just impossible for many school  districts like the six foot rule is  great  it's not even a perfect rule but it's  not realistic for many schools so  perhaps we need to downgrade to the  three foot rule that the who set forth  perhaps we could move some classrooms  outdoors where we see less transmission  there's all these things that are being  pushed aside of the conversation  in favor of a political talking point  that really should not be  anywhere in this discussion politics  should mean the least  when it comes to opening schools because  at the end of the day whether you're an  independent republican democrat your  child is your child  and if you have a family member that has  children or has a teacher in their  family  you should care equally for them  communication is really important and i  think communication with teachers  is going to be ultimately important and  trying to educate them  with regards to what information we have  on  covid right now which like you said it's  very imperfect  and we're still learning and we need to  reassess but  i think to have have two groups suffer  needlessly you know children and  teachers  is wrong um i think and  we have to balance each of their each of  their  their needs and we have to reassess i  mean it's  it has to be a very balanced approach  let's take some time and talk about  the outliers in this situation you know  we've mentioned  social determinants of health we  mentioned lower socioeconomic school  districts  you know there are schools out there  that receive funding from property taxes  in very wealthy neighborhoods that can  you know put acrylic  across acrylic is that plastic  see-through covering you see in some of  the  stores and medical offices they could  put that across the entire classroom  and then there's some areas where that's  going to be completely impossible  there's some areas where students  rely on school for feeding their  children  some areas that require the schools so  that they can get to work themselves  because they don't have child care  options available to them  unless we bring all of these very  important pieces  of the conversation to the table we're  going to be leaving a significant  population of the u.s behind  and that's not good either i can easily  see two individuals  on this subject you know a teacher on  one end who's immunocompromised and is  worried about going back to school  and at the same time i could see a  parent in a low  socioeconomic neighborhood of a special  needs child  saying i need my child to go back to  school i can't care for them  without without going broke because i  need to go to work or i don't have the  ability to teach them because i'm not  trained in this  and both sides are right in what they're  saying  they both need to be heard but where do  you go from there  that's where i struggle myself even at  times well i don't think you're alone i  mean i think the whole country is  struggling with that i think there are a  lot of people  i mean if it was if it was easy  you know we wouldn't be having these  discussions i mean really we wouldn't if  everything was going  the way it should be going but there are  a lot of competing  demands and trying to prioritize one  over another sometimes is  is very difficult but uh we do have to  invest in our children  and you know the children are our future  people watch this conversation you and i  speak  they understand where both you and i are  coming from what is  the one line you want to leave them with  yeah you know i  um the things that we're doing aren't  hard  to prevent this and the one thing that i  if we work together you know we're going  to get through this a lot  easier and a lot quicker and so if  you're wearing a mask  if you're doing simple things like  trying to distance yourself  washing your hands not touching your  face staying  away if you have cold symptoms these are  very very simple things to do  there's a lot of controversy surrounding  these topics and a lot of pushback  on doing certain things because  it's not what we're used to it's not  what we feel comfortable with  this is going to be something that's  transient and the time that we invest  now  on prevention and spread it's going to  pay off in dividends in the future  because we'll be able to go back to work  sooner we'll be able to be comfortable  with having our kids in school  sooner without restrictions you know  there's a lot of things so the sooner  that we can implement these things  and be more uh uh work as a team  yeah that you know we're gonna get  through this a lot quicker  check out